# Project Context: Go-libp2p TouchCalc Backend

## Project Overview
This is a Go-based spreadsheet application backend (TouchCalc) that we're integrating with libp2p for peer-to-peer functionality. The project is based on converting a Python Flask SocialCalc application to Go with Gin framework.

## Technology Stack
- **Language**: Go 1.21+
- **Web Framework**: Gin
- **Databases**: MongoDB, MySQL (to be added), AWS S3 (optional)
- **P2P**: go-libp2p
- **Session Management**: In-memory sessions
- **Authentication**: bcrypt password hashing

## Project Goals
1. Get the existing Go backend running locally
2. Integrate MongoDB for spreadsheet storage
3. Integrate MySQL for spreadsheet storage
4. Implement multi-database abstraction layer
5. Fix login flow to match py-libp2p reference
6. Render SocialCalc spreadsheet UI after login
7. Integrate go-libp2p for P2P synchronization

## Code Style Guidelines
- Follow Go standard conventions (gofmt)
- Use meaningful variable names
- Add comments for complex logic
- Error handling: always check and handle errors
- Use interfaces for abstraction (StorageBackend)
- Keep handlers thin, business logic in services

## Directory Structure

go-backend-touch-calc/
├── cmd/server/ # Main application entry
├── internal/
│ ├── auth/ # Authentication logic
│ ├── config/ # Configuration
│ ├── handlers/ # HTTP handlers
│ ├── models/ # Data models
│ ├── session/ # Session management
│ └── storage/ # Storage backends (S3, MongoDB, MySQL)
├── pkg/
│ ├── middleware/ # HTTP middleware
│ └── utils/ # Utilities
├── web/
│ ├── static/ # Static files (JS, CSS)
│ └── templates/ # HTML templates
└── go-libp2p-node/ # P2P node implementation


## Reference Implementation
- **py-libp2p reference**: https://github.com/seetadev/c4gt-website
- Review how login flow works in main.py
- Static files structure in static/ directory
- Template structure in templates/ directory

## Development Approach
1. Start by commenting out AWS dependencies (SES, S3) if no credentials
2. Test each component individually before integration
3. Use docker-compose for database services
4. Implement storage backends one at a time
5. Test thoroughly before moving to next step

## Important Notes
- AWS SES code should be commented out for local development
- Focus on MongoDB and MySQL integration first
- libp2p integration comes after basic functionality works
- Always test login → spreadsheet view flow after changes

## When Asking for Help
- Provide specific error messages
- Share relevant code context
- Mention which step you're on
- Include logs if available
